#!/bin/sh
# 42 machines: make the Ghostty AppImage from ~/bin a normal app --
# ~/.local/bin/ghostty (so the star-wars-theme hook and the GNOME launcher find
# it) and its own icon in the user icon theme.
#
# chezmoi re-runs this whenever the AppImage list below changes, i.e. after
# dropping a new Ghostty-*.AppImage into ~/bin and running `chezmoi apply`.
# machine = {{ .machine }}
{{- if eq .machine "42" }}
{{-   range (glob (joinPath .chezmoi.homeDir "bin" "Ghostty-*.AppImage")) }}
# appimage: {{ . }}
{{-   end }}
set -eu

A=$(ls -1 "$HOME"/bin/Ghostty-*.AppImage 2>/dev/null | sort -V | tail -n 1)
[ -n "$A" ] || exit 0
chmod +x "$A"
mkdir -p "$HOME/.local/bin"
ln -sfn "$A" "$HOME/.local/bin/ghostty"

ICONS=$HOME/.local/share/icons/hicolor/256x256/apps
T=$(mktemp -d "$HOME/.cache/ghostty-icon.XXXXXX" 2>/dev/null || mktemp -d)
# Only the icons, not the whole image (the 42 home quota is small). The name
# inside differs between AppImage builds, so fall back to .DirIcon.
for pat in '*.png' .DirIcon; do
    (cd "$T" && "$A" --appimage-extract "$pat" >/dev/null 2>&1) || true
done
ICON=$(find "$T" -name 'com.mitchellh.ghostty*.png' -size +1k -printf '%s %p\n' 2>/dev/null \
    | sort -n | tail -n 1 | cut -d' ' -f2-)
if [ -z "$ICON" ] && [ -e "$T/squashfs-root/.DirIcon" ]; then
    ICON=$(readlink -f "$T/squashfs-root/.DirIcon")
fi
if [ -n "$ICON" ]; then
    mkdir -p "$ICONS"
    cp -f "$ICON" "$ICONS/com.mitchellh.ghostty.png"
    gtk-update-icon-cache -q -t "$HOME/.local/share/icons/hicolor" 2>/dev/null || true
    printf '  \033[36m::\033[0m ghostty: %s, icon installed\n' "$(basename "$A")"
fi
rm -rf "${T:?}"
update-desktop-database -q "$HOME/.local/share/applications" 2>/dev/null || true
{{- end }}
